 was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Des Moines has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Des Moines' climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Des Moines has set <strong class=\"sig\">14 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 66 that chance alone would give, and <strong>85 record highs</strong>, more than the 66 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (76). Des Moines' all-time high is 110\u00b0F, on August 4, 1918.","t_chg":"Des Moines has warmed, days and nights alike","p_chg":"Across 147 complete years: Des Moines' average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.16\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.10 to +0.22), about +2.3\u00b0F since 1879. Des Moines' average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.15\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.09 to +0.23), about +2.3\u00b0F since 1879.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 7 of 12 months. Highs rose in 5 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Des Moines has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Des Moines' yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.30 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.02 to +0.57), about +4.3 inches since 1879. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 36.6 inches a year. The wettest year was 1881 (56.81 in); the driest, 1956 (17.07 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 9 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}